ve been able to see a draft  drastic decline in the cost of a policy  but despite our best efforts to find  ways within the system to reduce costs  for british columbians we've gone back  to icbc once again because of again the  strong financial position they find  themselves in as we come to the end of  the current fiscal year and we've been  advised by the board and the executive  that we'll be able to provide  a one-time relief payment of 110 dollars  to personal drivers and up to 165  dollars for commercial transportation  these small contributions will be large  for many people across british columbia  and we're very grateful for the hard  work at icbc from the staff  and from those policyholders who have  reduced prangs and we've also seen of  course increase dollars through  investments and minister farnworth can  talk about uh the robust position of the  corporation at this time but we're  confident that these one-time  contributions of 110 to individuals and  165 to businesses are timely to help  ease some of the pressure on pocketbooks  when it comes to  gas prices this is again directly a  result of international war in europe  that has disrupted supply chains and  disrupted commodity prices i've looked  at other jurisdictions across the  country the only other provinces that  have public auto insurance  opportunities like this  saskatchewan manitoba saskatchewan has  taken  advantage of that and provided a similar  rebate to their drivers others have  chosen to address taxation and i've  talked about this  at some length but i think the public  needs to know that the amount of money  that they pay in taxes at the pump is on  the pump when you pull up you know every  time you fill up what portion is going  to translink if you live in the lower  mainland what portion is going to bc  transit if you live outside of  the lower mainland and in areas where  public transit is provided and you also  know that the the amount that we pay for  carbon taxes here in british columbia go  right back to people and to businesses  to drive down emissions by ensuring that  we can do retrofit programs and other  initiatives to make life more affordable  and reduce our impact on the environment  we were reluctant and in fact would not  amend the taxation because it would just  be filled by an increase in prices at  the pump and we saw that in alberta  where the government promised a 13 cent  tax reduction on a monday and on a  wednesday the price went up 14 cents we  believe this is the best way to get  money into people's pockets and i'll  pass it over to minister farnworth to  provide some of the details  thank you premier and good morning  everyone i'd like to begin by  acknowledging that i'm joining you from  merit on the and the lands of the uh  kaplan people  it's been two years since the kobit 19  pandemic disrupted our lives  during that time british columbians have  proven time and again how resilient they  are  we've experienced the impacts of global  supply chain issues and how how here in  bc we are affected by world events  the russian invasion of ukraine has  resulted in an unprecedented increase in  the price of gas and other day-to-day  goods  today we're here to announce relief  since may of 2021 icbc's new enhanced  care model of insurance has lowered  premiums while ensuring better care  the majority of customers renewing their  full personal auto insurance under  enhanced care have saved on average 490  dollars per year  this follows two cobit 19 rebates issued  by icbc last year that provided drivers  with an average of 300 combined  i'm pleased that we are able to issue a  relief rebate this spring  drivers with personal policies will  receive a rebate of 110  while commercial customers who generally  incur higher expenses will receive a  rebate of 165 dollars  it's a total of more than 395 million  dollars going directly back to the  people in british columbia  people will begin with to receive their  rebates starting in may  this is one more way that this  government is making life more  affordable for people  thanks very much  uh thank you as a reminder to media on  the phone line please press star one to  enter the queue you're limited to one  question and one follow-up first  question today is from rob shaw check  news  oh hi premier uh i know that translink  yesterday announced it's raising fares  uh 2.3 percent and i think there's an  argument some people have made of why  not use  some of this money for public transit  here in victoria or in vancouver lower  the prices there rather than give it to  motorists directly  make a more economical or better overall  solution through public transit did you  think about that or why wasn't that part  of this  well we did look at the taxes at the  pump which is uh certainly  there are people in the community who  feel that this would be a more  appropriate way to act but we've had  advice from  ubc economists and others who have  advised us that a reduction in taxes at  the pump would only be replaced by an  increase in the commodity price  similarly we believe that translink and  bc transit are operating in the public  interest they need to manage their fleet  they need to manage their fare box  to complement the work that are the the  resources that they can get through uh  gas taxes and any direct uh  contributions from orders of government  uh that's how it's been operating we  believe that uh this is the best way  forward to ensure that we're addressing  challenges in areas where there is no  access to public transit which is a big  big chunk of real estate from  outside of the lower mainland throughout  most of uh northern interior british  columbia  we had uh certainly loud heard loudly  and clearly that there are many people  who do not have the opportunity to avail  themselves of a world-class transit  system as we have  in our metropolitan areas so this we  felt was the best way to ensure that we  could protect those  drivers and also those who would uh have  inflationary pressures brought to bear  as a result of uh vladimir putin's war  in europe  rob do you have a follow-up  sure thanks uh i know your government  has said in the past many times that the  bc liberals used icbc like a piggy bank  taking profits out of it to spend on  things when it was in a strong financial  position  rather than letting the corporation use  the money internally to prepare for the  ups and downs of an insurance company  are you not doing just the same thing  here using icbc as a piggy bank to  address a a problem and taking money  away from it no well and i don't believe  we are we brought in legislation to  prevent future governments from dipping  into reserves at icbc to pad their  budgets what we're doing here and what  minister farnworth has explained for the  two previous covet  benefits we're giving money back to the  policyholders the people who finance  icbc this is a rebate based on the  robust position that the corporation is  in and that full marks uh to the  executive and the management team as  well as the board uh for transforming  what was a dumpster fire  aptly called  into a corporation that is in a position  to provide relief to the people who  funded the policyholders and perhaps  minister farnworth can can add to that  but this is this is apples and oranges  comparisons this is about giving the  money back to the people who put it in  the pot not taking out of the pot to  meet political uh needs within  government please go ahead minister  oh thank you and just just to follow up  on that so  icbc's significantly stronger uh  financial position than was predicted at  the beginning of the uh of the fiscal  year uh this year uh and as a result  it's not only able to meet its capital  requirements uh but also allows us in  the uh in the case of uh of of this  particular year to be able as the  premier said to rebate uh some of that  money back to the policyholders uh the  ones who are uh you know financing the  the company through through their  insurance their insurance policies  uh it's not going back to general  revenue it is going back uh to the to  the individual policyholders  next question is from cole schizler  black press  are you there cole  okay sorry hi can you hear me now yeah  yeah thanks for taking my question um so  110  for the average driver depending on  where you live that's  about a tank and a half a gas maybe less  um  how far is this hundred and ten dollars  really going to go to make life more  affordable for british columbia  well i think it's a significant  contribution at a very difficult time  for drivers as they look at the price at  the pump to know that there's relief on  the way i think is is going to be  positive news  i appreciate that government can't  buffet or protect consumers from runaway  prices  other than using the tools that are at  our disposal to reduce costs and in this  instance we're able to provide a  an amount of money back to policyholders  to drivers to help them along the way i  believe that it'll be welcomed  it is again by comparison to the uh the  the move in alberta which won't take  effect until april first it was based on  the price of the commodity and it was  going to be a direct tax reduction which  would work out to about five bucks a  week  so 110 dollars uh in a one-time fund or  five bucks a week as uh the alberta  situation uh is going to manifest itself  i think we'll be welcomed certainly uh  on border in border communities where  there's an opportunity to uh  to shop across the line uh we want to of  course keep people in british columbia  but uh consumers uh are driven by uh  price and the price point they'll make  those choices those personal choices and  uh and we'll leave them to do that  call do you have a follow-up  yeah um  sorry i i lost my question of it there  um if prices skyrocket further if we go  back to the you know over 220 mark at  the pump does the government have  further measures that you can take and  can you shed light on what further  measures might be at the government's  disposal well cole uh we're making an  announcement today we have capacity to  make other announcements in the future  but we're monitoring inflationary  pressures not just at the pump but  across  the economy and uh we're always ready to  step in if the need arises but today's  announcement stands on its own uh it  will assist businesses it will assist  drivers and we have other tools at our  disposal that we're looking at for other  interventions as  the spring unfolds and we get into the  summer we do not know what the  consequences of vladimir putin's  aggression in ukraine a war in ukraine  will have on the global economy the  global marketplace so all governments  not just british columbia and i and i  think this is a a caution for people who  are tuning in right now and hearing me  uh speak that we are not out of the  woods yet we are in for a series of  a time of instability here in in british  columbia and in canada and indeed around  the world and so we want to ensure that  we're able to respond uh the minister of  finance uh looked at this very carefully  through our processes we didn't act  swiftly as some will argue i'm sure i  can i can almost read the liberal press  release now but we acted prudently we  made sure that we waited until near the  end of the fiscal year so that icbc  could have a clear picture of what their  financial situation was that's a  responsible thing to do  i pride  our government on our ability to be  prudent be focused but also at the  center of everything we do have people  in mind so as the summer  progresses as the spring progresses into  summer if there are further inflationary  pressures we'll be working to try and  find ways to reduce costs for people  next question is from yvonne raymond ctv  vancouver island  hi i'm just wondering how you came up  with a hundred and ten dollars for  personal vehicles and 165  for commercial what's uh what's the um i  guess significance behind the exact  number  i'll pass that to minister farnworth to  respond uh it's a direct result of the  financial situation at icbc and he can  shed some light on that go ahead  minister  yeah thank you uh thank you for the  question uh it's exactly looking at the  uh the financial situation of icbc in  terms of what was projected at the the  beginning of the year uh finding the  balance between what icbc required in  terms of its capital uh requirements and  what uh potentially would be available  uh to uh to provide in the form of a  relief rebate to policyholders that's  how we came up with the uh the uh the  number uh and it's about uh  395 400 million dollars that would be  available and then recognizing that  commercial vehicles uh  are on the road more have more x more uh  expenses uh and so  the 110 and 165 dollars was achieved uh  on that basis uh as a way of ensuring  that everybody is treated equitably but  also recognizing from the commercial  vehicle perspective that they do have  some higher higher operating costs  follow-up yvonne  no thank you  thank you next question is from von  palmer vancouver son  good day premier good day minister um  if icbc can afford to give this kind of  money back to policyholders  doesn't that suggest that icbc was  overcharging  and should have been offering these  rebates  this month in any event  well i'll again i'll let mike answer the  substance of that bond but  the available  resource to do this as a result largely  of investments that icbc makes as you  know to  ensure that they have uh appropriate  funds to manage any eventuality going  forward and the instability  caused in the marketplace has led to  an increase in value for commodities  that icbc may invest in but but mike can  give you more detail on that but it's uh  it wasn't about too many policy dollars  in the pot it was about uh having a a  positive year-end as a result of  investments that the corporation makes  on behalf of policyholders go ahead  minister  yeah no thank you i'm just following up  on that uh what we've seen since the  reduction of enhanced care was the  reduction in rates  on average of about 20 percent uh the  rates being frozen in place  working through the utilities commission  so for three years of stability rate  stability um that has allowed with the  uh as the premier stated the investment  returns that icbc has received to not  only deal with the financial position  that it was in uh to significantly move  to rebuilding the the capital reserves  for the uh the minimum capital test  that's required in the planets in place  around that and recognizing that because  of the uh the good financial management  there has been uh this ability to  provide uh a one-time uh  rebate based on the performance of the  icbc over the uh over the past year  von do you have a follow-up  yes please uh premiere uh you and bruce  ralston have both referred to a pending  report from the bc utilities commission  with its recommendations on how  to proceed  to manage or otherwise deal with  gasoline prices um and several times  referred to that it's it's coming soon  um what what can people when first of  all when can we expect to see that  report and  what can people expect  that it will lead to uh well vaughn i'm  not aware of uh the timing of the report  uh nor am i uh  aware of the content but i can say  broadly to drivers one of the  initiatives that we brought forward as  you know well was to bring forward  legislation to ensure that the  commission had the ability to look into  uh unusual increases in in the price at  the pump uh to ensure that there was an  explanation as to why the prices were  going up that allows uh customers uh  british columbians to have confidence  that there's no gouging going on you've  been covering this issue for a long long  time and you know that whenever there's  a spike  in prices there's an immediate uh  expectation that there's gouging going  on uh  the uh the anti-tax crowd says it's all  about taxes the taxes stayed the same  the price of the commodity goes up and  so allowing the utilities commission  that those who have expertise in the  field to review these increases and to  determine why the price went up how it  was being  declined how it will decline over time i  think is useful for the traveling public  and more importantly it's it's useful  for all british columbians whether they  uh  drive a a car or not to know that we're  not being gouged because of uh  challenges uh in other markets so i i  think that the the review will be useful  for government to understand what's  happening in the marketplace not from  a partisan perspective but from an  independent perspective which is what  the uh  the people at the commission can provide  for not just government for british  columbia  keith baldrick global news  oh hi was that me yes please  uh yes so uh  given the robust financial health of uh  icbc  uh it begs the question why wasn't the  rebate even bigger it wasn't just  investment income in icbc that has a a  better bottom line also claims are  dropping dramatically uh was the  expectation perhaps a little out there  that this rebate would have been higher  than 110 rick mcanlis former civil  servant who has intervener status at the  utilities commission his analysis  suggested he could afford at least 125  dollars this year  thank you for the question uh and i know  rick uh and he i i know that he is among  uh a handful of experts on this uh this  issue and uh i i certainly value his his  input uh but i have to say and and you  will know this uh keith that uh finance  ministers certainly for the past five  years have looked withdread at the  bottom line at icbc because it swings uh  so much in short periods of time and we  wanted to wait till the right near the  end of the fiscal year so we could have  certainty that the corporation did have  a robust bottom line and i have been in  this job now for five years and at this  time of the year i have seen more often  than not  the situation at icbc deteriorate over a  matter of days to a point where it's had  a material impact on our budget so  we wanted to make sure we were following  this and tracking it to the last  possible moment to give relief to  drivers but also to ensure that we were  not putting the corporation back into  the dumpster and mike can uh  talk about the uh reduction in accidents  and and what that means for the the  vitality of the corporation  yeah no we have seen a reduction in an  accident certainly through the uh the  period of kobit uh but we are coming out  of covid and we are seeing more and more  people on the road uh people getting out  to you know to  travel again in a way that they haven't  been able to do for the last few years  and so it's been critical that we try  and maintain that balance between  recognizing the strong financial  position that icbc has found itself in  at the year end with a recognition that  while claims have gone down during the  cobit period the reality is is we are  moving out of that uh and we we may well  see uh uh you know a potential increase  so it's a it's that balance between  ensuring uh this recognizing the strong  financial position  what icbc requires and um  the uh the the the surplus um or the the  unexpected to the the windfall if you  like uh that we can return to  policyholders in the form of a uh  relief rebate  keith you have a follow-up  yeah given again the the dramatic  turnaround in icbc finances and in the  fiscal plan itself the three-year fiscal  plan projects more modest surpluses in  the next couple years but  is it realistic for policyholders now to  expect rebates  each and every year  that's a good question and we have been  fortunate as minister farmworth has said  uh to have uh surpluses that were  sufficient to provide  funds back to the policyholders and that  was welcome news  and this i'm sure will be welcome news  as well  but uh we want to make sure that we're  taking these steps as they come rather  than looking too far down the road we  want to protect the hard work of drivers  and the  the team at icbc to get it back into a  position where we can make decisions  like this and taking advantage too soon  of that positive situation could well  imperil any more success going forward  we want to make sure rates stay low  and and the way to do that is to allow  the corporation to make these decisions  in consultation with the board and the  government and uh and that's what we've  been doing we'll keep on that track uh  always mindful that uh  again from our own personal experience  much less the experience of drivers over  the past decade uh price the cost of car  insurance was unsustainable for many  individuals and families we've been able  to turn that around because of hard work  by the corporation and by the team that  we sent in to find a way forward we want  to make sure we don't lose track of that  our last question today comes from mira  bain cbc  okay uh premier i know you mentioned  that you'll be monitoring gas prices in  the situation um uh that's happening in  ukraine but i'm wondering do you have  any sort of a hard date when you'll be  reviewing um  gas prices again you know this is a  one-time um  only uh rebate right now but could we  see another rebate in the future well we  do have as i said to in response to  another question there are other  mechanisms that we're looking at we did  look at but we have we have a  challenging period ahead of us as i said  the instability and the aggression of  vladimir putin is is going to be  affecting us for some period of time not  just at the pumps but at the  at the checkout at the grocery store and  a host of other areas and we want to  make sure uh that we're monitoring that  and uh ensuring that we have the  resources to respond uh when required  i have been engaging with my colleagues  the premiers across the country  on this issue we came uh we we were  driven by consensus the council of the  federation we weren't able to reach  consensus on how we would approach the  federal government uh to see what steps  they could take  but i do know i'm meeting with uh the  prime minister next week and  affordability inflation the consequences  that we'll have on a range of of issues  that are before our government and  before the people of bc will be a very  high priority  mayor do you have a follow-up  yes um and so this is a question just on  another front for the premiere um we're  just wondering whether  um ndp leader jagmeet singh sought  advice from you regarding the confidence  and the supply agreement um you know did  he talk to you about it at all and what  advice did you give him if any well we  did we did talk about it uh early on uh  shortly after the election and and  before the uh the agreement was  announced earlier this week  i'm pleased to say that  the work that we did andrew weaver and i  to create a  casa agreement here in british columbia  to provide stable government through our  minority period  has been duplicated in other  jurisdictions uconn is using a similar  model for their minority parliament  right now  when there were minority governments in  new brunswick and newfoundland i spoke  to not just the premiers but the  opposition leaders about how we were  able to find a way to cooperate and i  did have that conversation with the  prime minister as well as with judgment  singh i believe that what british  columbians and canadians want is for  elected representatives to cooperate and  collaborate to to get outcomes for them  only the most fierce of partisans and  you've seen this in the response to the  agreement that was reached by mr sang  and mr trudeau only the fiercest of  partisans would want to continue  fighting an election that was concluded  last fall what canadians want is for  their elected representatives wherever  they come from whatever their political  stripe roll up your sleeves and work for  us get things done for us and i believe  the confidence and supply agreement that  we had here in british columbia as well  as those that have followed will set the  template for how minority governments  work now and into the future and i would  hope  that the success of these approaches  would allow majority governments to be  more flexible in their engagements with  opposition parties and i would also hope  that opposition parties would see the  opportunity to be partners in the  delivery of programs rather than just  adversaries obviously we need in our  system to have an adversarial  relationship in the legislature or the  house of commons so that we can get  better outcomes but i think you can also  get better outcomes from cooperating  rather than just punching each other in  the face  that's all the time we have this morning  thank you for joining us thanks  you

